content.text:
This is clear-cut — the file was newly created in 878f010 with a syntax error (a bare `if` block directly inside an anonymous class body, missing the enclosing `performHighlight` method override), which wouldn't even compile. The fix commit 04139db just wraps that same code in a proper `protected void performHighlight(Highlight h, MotionEvent e)` method override.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 878f01068593e4f72334452100d346ccd050f1e3

## Reasoning
(1) I checked git history for `CustomPieChart.java` (the only file touched by the fix commit) and found only two commits ever touched it: the fix itself (04139db) and its immediate predecessor (878f010), which created the file from scratch.

(2) In 878f010, the new anonymous subclass of `PieRadarChartTouchListener` contains an `if` statement floating directly in the class body, not inside any method:
```java
mChartTouchListener = new PieRadarChartTouchListener(this){
    if (h != null && !h.equalTo(mLastHighlighted)) {
        ...
    }
};
```
This is invalid Java — a class body cannot contain a bare statement outside a method, so this would fail to compile (matching "fix ci error" as the follow-up commit message). The fix wraps this code in `protected void performHighlight(Highlight h, MotionEvent e) { ... }`, correctly overriding the intended method to disable unhighlighting.

(3) Evidence: `git log` shows the file has exactly two commits in its entire history, and the diff of 878f010 shows it introducing the malformed syntax verbatim, which 04139db then repairs by adding the missing method signature — no other logic changed. This is a straightforward compile-error bug, introduced entirely in the single commit that created the file.

